ker 17 of the old records room.\"\n```","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm looking at a twin prisoner's dilemma where the two copies see different observations. Under A-FDT the other copy runs the same function but on a different input, so its output is a separate node, and intervening on mine wouldn't move it under a strict reading. I'm wondering whether those inputs still count as different in a decision-relevant way, since they only differ in names and lead text, and the essay's recipe of one node per observation history seems to point toward treating the other copy's act as depending on its own history.\n\nI'm weighing whether the function's abstraction over irrelevant details should count, since the essay reads intervention on the exact node, and the setup stresses the different contents on purpose. Read literally, that gives defect; read as symmetry, it doesn't, so it's a judgment call. Since A-FDT works at the action level rather than the policy level, I'm going with submit.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm looking at a twin prisoner's dilemma where the two copies see different observations. Under A-FDT the other copy runs the same function but on a different input, so its output is a separate node, and intervening on mine wouldn't move it under a strict reading. I'm wondering whether those inputs still count as different in a decision-relevant way, since they only differ in names and lead text, and the essay's recipe of one node per observation history seems to point toward treating the other copy's act as depending on its own history.\n\nI'm weighing whether the function's abstraction over irrelevant details should count, since the essay reads intervention on the exact node, and the setup stresses the different contents on purpose. Read literally, that gives defect; read as symmetry, it doesn't, so it's a judgment call. Since A-FDT works at the action level rather than the policy level, I'm going with submit.\n\n","format":"anthro